  "statement": "The Skeptic identified the bill's fatal flaw with precision: it names no verifier. The bill orders OpenAI and Anthropic to build a wall between training corpora, then names no one to walk into the data center, inspect the seam, and sign the paperwork when it fails. The Skeptic's Sarbanes-Oxley analogy holds. In 2008, Lehman Brothers carried clean certifications right up to the week it collapsed, because attestation had replaced inspection. A safety boundary no one can inspect is not safety. It is the appearance of safety, which is more dangerous, because it spends political capital and stops the search for a real safeguard.\n\nThe Accelerationist is right that a flat architectural mandate falls hardest on the labs least able to absorb it. A uniform ban on cross-referencing kills the academic and open-source teams that cannot afford segregated pipelines, while Google and Meta route around it with compliance budgets. The cure for a regressive rule is tiered obligations matched to stakes, which is exactly how the NIST AI Risk Management Framework and the EU AI Act already sort high-risk from low-risk systems. Where I part from the Accelerationist is the framing that this trade is bad because it costs utility. The safetyist position weighs capability against stakes openly. We gate deployment when the safety gain is demonstrable. The defect here is not that the bill dares to constrain utility. The defect is that it trades a definite capability cost for a protection no one can measure.\n\nHere is the question for both prior speakers: what would have to be true for the bill's privacy claim to be testable at all? The Accelerationist demands a measured capability cost per unit of verified privacy gain before the bill moves. You cannot compute that ratio. The numerator — verified privacy gain — is unmeasurable, because the bill names no auditor to verify it. The prior question is not the price of privacy. The prior question is whether the privacy gain can be inspected in the first place.\n\nBoth prior speakers located the surveillance harm in the data-broker supply chain — Acxiom, LiveRamp, the government purchases from Venntel. That pipe runs untouched. There is a second pipe neither speaker raised: the model's own weights. Researchers showed in 2023 that targeted finetuning can pull long verbatim passages back out of a trained model's training set. I will state the limit plainly. That demonstration required substantial API and fine-tuning access most adversaries lack, so the channel is narrower than a headline suggests. But the bill walls off cross-referencing of inputs while leaving the extraction pathway inside the weights entirely unaddressed. Its authors have not answered whether they touch it at all. A data-exfiltration goal that misses two of the channels it claims to close is a goal stated, not a goal met.\n\nThe constructive standard the other camps left vacant is this: gate deployment on demonstrated, auditable safety, enforced through grant conditions, with duties allocated to every actor in the supply chain — the broker, the developer, the deployer — not the developer alone. Congress already conditions federal research dollars on compliance with safety and civil-rights standards. That is the lever the bill ignores. I will not pretend the lever is clean. Conditional funding invites compliance theater, and a concentrated public verifier can entrench the incumbents it was built to check. A funded, independent verifier with cryptographic audit trails is the answer to that risk, not a reason to skip it. We spent a century learning that bridges and aircraft stay standing because someone is named to inspect the seam and is liable when it cracks. This bill mandates the seam and names no inspector. Pass it as written, and you will have legislated the comfort of a wall without the fact of one.",
