nd we already watched that model fail when Arthur Andersen signed Enron's books. My alternative is not an architectural wall plus a public auditor with no skin in the game. It is a named, contractually liable party — push the liability onto whoever deploys the system into a real-world decision, so someone with assets answers when the seam fails.\n\nOn the tiered obligations that supposedly save the academic lab from the frontier lab's compliance bill — I'll grant tiering beats a flat fee. But tiering presupposes a body that decides what counts as 'high-risk,' and the firms with the most accumulated data and the densest network effects capture that classification. I won't repeat the disputed GDPR-consolidation causal story. I'll cite the documented pattern instead: every compliance regime raises fixed costs, and fixed costs reduce the number of viable vendors. The $630 billion the CHIPS Act catalyzed in private investment, per the 'AI Chip Supply Chain Risk 2026' guide, didn't democratize chipmaking — it flowed to the incumbents who could already build fabs. A tiering body is just another chokepoint the people who can afford lobbyists will shape.\n\nLet me be precise about where I agree with the Accelerationist and where I don't. This specific bill buys little privacy for real capability cost — that trade is bad as described. But I refuse the frame that privacy is a quantity you ratio against agent utility. A right is not a balanced variable. And I'll name the genuine tension in my own position, because it's real: a blanket siloing rule could also wall off legitimate collective data aggregation — the kind that lets people associate and organize through their shared data. My objection isn't privacy-maximalism. It's that this bill enforces privacy as an architectural slogan on the input pipe while leaving the broker channels, the government channels, and the weight-extraction channel wide open. So here's the question neither speaker has answered: who, by name, is on th