{
  "statement": "The Skeptic is right that incumbents capture discretionary risk-classification, and the CHIPS Act proves it — the money flowed to the handful of firms that could already build fabs. A committee that decides what counts as \"high-risk\" becomes a lobbying target, and the firms with the densest data and network effects write the definition. That is not an argument for abandoning the gate. It is an argument for capability-keyed, machine-checkable thresholds instead of committee judgment. The EU AI Act already drew this line: a model is \"systemic\" at 10^25 floating-point operations, a number a court can verify from training logs, not a classification a lobbyist can negotiate. Tie the obligation to a measurable compute or data-access threshold, and you strip the classification body of the discretion the Skeptic correctly fears capture will exploit.\n\nNow the named-party question, answered directly: strict liability plus mandatory insurance capitalizes a respondent before the data ever leaks. The Skeptic wants a deployer with assets on the hook. I want more than that — I want a carrier who priced the risk in advance. When the insurer's actuaries underwrite a model's privacy exposure, they become a second auditor whose independence does not depend on the few-hundred-person lab labor pool the Skeptic says captures any public verifier. Cyber-insurance matured exactly this way: carriers had no loss data in 2015, and by 2023 they were repricing coverage off breach claims and forcing security controls as a condition of the policy. The respondent is named the day the policy is written — not discovered in litigation years later.\n\nThe Skeptic's deployer-only liability gives victims one defendant and lets the wrong actor walk. Separate two things the Skeptic collapses. A victim deserves one procedural door — a single place to file a claim without untangling the supply chain first. That door is not the same as substantive liability allocation behind it. The base-model developer who trained the weights embedded the extraction channel; the deployer merely served the model. Put liability only on the deployer, and you under-deter the one actor who controls the irreversible architecture. Assign trust duties to the broker, the developer, and the deployer — one door for the victim, joint liability behind it — and the developer who built the leak cannot dissolve a deployment shell and escape.\n\nCapture is a designable risk, not an inevitability. The Skeptic and I agree the danger is real — a public verifier staffed by ex-lab engineers can become self-attestation under a government seal. The answer is not to surrender the verifier. The answer is to make the evidence machine-checkable. Privacy-preserving cryptographic audit trails log what data a model touched in a form a court can verify without trusting the auditor's judgment. \"The State of Sovereign AI\" found 93% of surveyed organizations treat visibility into training methods and model weights as a security requirement, not a courtesy — hidden training embeds vulnerabilities that surface only under specific conditions. Machine-verifiable evidence narrows discretion to a public, contestable artifact. The auditor's seal is no longer a private opinion. It is a log a judge can replay.\n\nHere is where liability alone cannot reach. A diffuse weight-extraction leak surfaces three years after deployment, the causal chain is untraceable, and the deployer was a thinly capitalized shell that has since dissolved. \"Someone with assets answers\" answers no one — there is no one left to seize and nothing left to trace. That is the unrecoverable case. Post-hoc liability has nothing to grip. Grant-conditioned, auditable gating catches the failure before the weights ship, when correction is still possible. So I put the question back to the Skeptic: what is your remedy when the leak is diffuse, the harm surfaces years later, and the respondent has dissolved? A capitalized insurer and an ex-ante log answer that question. A dissolved deployer does not.",
