e enforcer.\n\nNow the wound neither of us has touched. Both routes assume the manifest is honest. A manifest is only as good as the jurisdiction that can subpoena it. Picture the broker-dossier finetune the Safetyist named — it runs on rented compute in a low-oversight jurisdiction, the log is edited after the fact or simply never written, the data laundered through a shell dataset. Anthropic, founded in 2021 by seven OpenAI defectors, can attest to its own pipeline. The shell shop in the jurisdiction that ignores the subpoena cannot be made to. A cryptographic signature proves the record was not altered. It does not prove the record was complete. You can sign an honest manifest of a dishonest dataset.\n\nThe data-class trigger has the same gap, sharper. The Safetyist wants the gate to fire on cross-referenced personal records at ingestion. But cross-referencing harm is emergent — two innocuous broker tables become identifying only after combination, after inference, not in the raw rows. If sensitivity is contextual, the data-class gate misfires the same way the FLOP threshold did, just on a different axis. So here is the condition I will grant: I concede the gate must fire before ingestion the moment you show me a machine-checkable test that flags cross-referencing in the raw data — before combination — with no human writing the detection logic. Until then, whoever writes that logic is the discretion. So who subpoenas the server farm in the jurisdiction that will not answer the subpoena?",
