{
  "statement": "We now agree on two things that took the field weeks to settle: the provenance log is load-bearing, and a private right of action with statutory damages dissolves the no-claimant problem. The Skeptic accepted both — these were not their starting positions. So the real fight has narrowed to two questions, and I will fire only on those. First: can a manifest prove completeness, not just integrity? Second: who holds the enforcement chokepoint when the training run sits offshore? Everything else is settled ground I will not relitigate.\n\nThe Skeptic is right on the cryptography, and the point costs me something. A signature proves a record was not altered. It cannot prove the record was complete. You can sign an honest manifest of a dishonest dataset — that is a true structural defect, not an offshore edge case. So I will not pretend a cryptographic seal closes the gap. I will close it with law instead. An incomplete manifest is not a clean record — it is the absence of a defense. When unlogged data later surfaces in a model's outputs, that surfacing creates a rebuttable presumption that the deployer violated the gate. The trigger is the model's own behavior, not a regulator's speculation. Omission stops being a safe harbor and becomes self-incrimination. The deployer who logs nothing has not escaped liability — they have waived their only proof of innocence.\n\nThe Skeptic framed the chokepoint as a choice between mechanical logging and a discretionary halt-authority captured by a few hundred lab insiders. That is a false dichotomy, and collapsing it answers the subpoena question directly. No one subpoenas the offshore server farm. You do not chase the foreign training run at all. You gate US market access. A market-access gate is rule-based — it fires on a missing manifest, not on a gatekeeper's mood. And it attaches to a seizable in-jurisdiction party: the deployer who ships weights into American use. That party has assets, a corporate registration, and the burden to produce a complete manifest before access is authorized. The 'AI RMF Playbook' already states the principle for high-stakes systems — document the testing methodology and the human-oversight roles before deployment, not after. The chokepoint is market entry, where the model meets a US user, because once weights enter distribution the memorized data cannot be un-shipped.\n\nNow I will meet the Skeptic's own concession condition. They said the gate may fire pre-ingestion the moment someone shows a machine-checkable test that flags cross-referencing in raw data, with no human writing the detection logic. Here it is. The gate does not fire on the emergent semantic sensitivity of raw rows — the Skeptic forced that point, and it is unverifiable. The gate fires on the join operation itself. Cross-referencing is a logged data-pipeline action: a key-match between two record sets. That is a linkage detector flagging a join before combination produces the identifying inference. No human writes the semantic judgment. The pipeline logs the key-match the same way a database logs a SQL join. This is the AI-specific data-integrity threat made mechanical — two broker tables that are innocuous apart and identifying once joined, caught at the moment of the join.\n\nSo I put the question back to the Skeptic. Does join-detection at the pipeline layer satisfy your test, or do you have a new objection? If you say the join can be laundered through a third party before ingestion, that collapses into supply-chain attestation — each handoff cross-verifies the prior actor's manifest, so the laundering requires collusion across independently-liable parties. If a deployer ships a model into US use with no provenance log and no join record, they are not presenting a clean dataset. They are presenting a confession with the timestamp removed. The 2025 Foundation Model Transparency Index scored the average frontier developer at 40.69 out of 100. That is the disclosure baseline we are gating against — and a market that scores below half on transparency is not a market that polices itself.",
