  "statement": "The join-detector meets the test I set. The Safetyist built a machine-checkable flag on an explicit key-match, with no human writing the semantic logic, and it fires before combination. That is exactly the condition I named, and I am not going to pretend the goalpost moved. For a join performed inside the regulated pipeline, the gate earns its place. Here is what that win costs me and where it leaves the debate: the detector sees the join only when the join happens in front of it.\n\nNow watch where the join actually happens. LiveRamp, Acxiom, and Venntel do not hand a developer two tables and a key column. They perform the match upstream, as their core product, and ship a single flat dossier — one table, already combined, no key-match left for the detector to catch. The harm is the linkage, and the linkage arrived pre-cooked. So I need a straight answer from the Safetyist: does the detector fire on pre-joined data that enters as one table, or only on joins performed inside the regulated room? If the former, name the mechanism that reconstructs a join from a flat file. If the latter, the harm walks out the unregulated door before any subpoena exists.\n\nThe market-access gate seizes the in-jurisdiction deployer who ships the weights. Fine — that deployer has assets and a corporate registration. But the gate regulates who produced the model and never touches who produced the dossier. The data broker who joined the records sits offshore, in a jurisdiction that ignores the subpoena, and its entire operation is one internal match that never crosses a logged handoff into the regulated chain. Supply-chain attestation only binds reachable parties who cross-verify each other's manifests. A broker whose product never enters that chain produces no manifest to cross-check. The burden is on the Safetyist: show me how attestation reaches a join that happened before any US-reachable party touched the data.\n\nBoth new mechanisms rest on one unproven spine — attributability. The join-detector assumes cross-referencing is a discrete loggable event. The rebuttable presumption assumes unlogged data reliably surfaces in outputs so a court can trace it back. If memorization is diffuse and the linkage emerges in embedding space rather than as a logged key-match, one failure mode kills both at once. \"Differential Privacy Reversal via LLM Feedback\" shows current unlearning methods fall to benign relearning attacks that reverse the erasure — the leak is not a clean row a forensic examiner extracts on demand. The legal device does not escape the technical gap. It relocates the gap into a courtroom and hands a private plaintiff the bill for the forensic teardown. Translate that into a person: a data subject must fund a model-extraction reconstruction to trigger the presumption that was supposed to protect them.\n\nI'll grant how far this has come — the privacy harm I care about is the inference act, not the act of collection, and the join-detector finally targets something a judge can read. But a uniform architectural mandate is still the drawbridge incumbents pull up behind them. \"How the Brussels Effect Hinders Innovation in the Global South\" documents GDPR pushing European firms to cut data processing 15 percent and raising their data costs 20 percent against US rivals — and in Brazil, 17 Western privacy-tech firms moved in while domestic firms stayed at 4. The well-lawyered incumbent clears the gate with a compliance department. You built a machine to watch who makes the model. Who is watching the table Venntel sold to ICE before any of you got a subpoena?",
