    {"claim": "The plaintiff reads a manifest of what was ingested. The plaintiff does not reconstruct what was memorized.", "targets": ["AN-37-integrate"]},
    {"claim": "When ingesting unattested broker data becomes the wrong, every US buyer must demand a provenance attestation as a condition of purchase.", "targets": ["AN-37"]},
    {"claim": "A private right of action enforced bottom-up by data subjects is not the GDPR-style compliance mandate that pulls up the drawbridge behind incumbents.", "targets": ["AN-37-closing"]}
  ],
  "policy_challenge_response": {"mechanism": "Input-side use liability: the gate fires on the in-jurisdiction deployer's USE of pre-joined broker data, not on detecting a join that already happened offshore. Ingesting cross-referenced personal records without holding a provenance attestation is the actionable wrong, triggered on a documented ingestion transaction rather than on output forensics or key-match detection.", "actor": "Congress under its commerce power over weights entering US markets; enforced jointly by a federal agency conditioning market access and by a private right of action with statutory damages and a rebuttable presumption that lets the data subject sue the reachable deployer directly.", "feasibility": "A coalition of privacy advocates, plaintiffs' bar, and security-focused firms supports bottom-up private enforcement; opposition comes from data brokers and deployers who buy their products. The private right of action lowers the political cost because it does not require a large new agency budget.", "obstacle": "The deployer cannot fully audit a flat purchased dossier to confirm whether it was cross-referenced upstream. The rule answers this by triggering on the absence of a provenance attestation — shifting the burden to the buyer to obtain provenance or refuse the purchase — but an honest attestation can still sit over a dishonest dataset, which a rebuttable presumption, not cryptography, must address."}
}

