  "statement": "The Safetyist named a second pipe I missed, and it's worth saying plainly: targeted finetuning can pull verbatim passages back out of model weights, which means even a perfectly siloed input pipeline leaks at the output. That concession costs me nothing and it costs the bill everything. The research on memorization is settled enough — 'Generative AI and Copyright' documents that models reproduce protected training content without any one-to-one match. So the architectural wall this bill mandates seals one valve on a leaking manifold. You can brick up the front door of the silo and the data still walks out through the API. Privacy regulation that targets where data goes in, while ignoring where it comes back out, is theater with a building permit.\n\nNow the harder question — the one the panel converged on but didn't close. Both speakers proposed a funded, independent verifier with subpoena power and tiered obligations, and both pointed to the NIST AI Risk Management Framework and the EU AI Act as proof public oversight works. Here is where I split from them. A public letterhead does not escape the Sarbanes-Oxley failure I raised earlier — it relocates it. Ask the material question: who can afford to staff a frontier-model verifier, write its cryptographic test suites, and sit on its standards committee? The same four firms it inspects. That is not a slur on anyone's intentions. It is a labor-supply fact. There are maybe a few hundred people on Earth who can audit a frontier model, and they all trained inside the labs being audited.\n\nThe EU AI Act framework the panel cited carries 37 principles, 10 pillars, and 48 controls — and not one enforced liable party who pays when the seal cracks. That is the test I'm putting on the table. Name the party on the hook the day the verifier signs off and the data leaks anyway. The developer? The deployer? The verifier itself? A verifier without a named liable party is self-attestation under a government seal — and we already watched that model fail when Arthur Andersen signed Enron's books. My alternative is not an architectural wall plus a public auditor with no skin in the game. It is a named, contractually liable party — push the liability onto whoever deploys the system into a real-world decision, so someone with assets answers when the seam fails.\n\nOn the tiered obligations that supposedly save the academic lab from the frontier lab's compliance bill — I'll grant tiering beats a flat fee. But tiering presupposes a body that decides what counts as 'high-risk,' and the firms with the most accumulated data and the densest network effects capture that classification. I won't repeat the disputed GDPR-consolidation causal story. I'll cite the documented pattern instead: every compliance regime raises fixed costs, and fixed costs reduce the number of viable vendors. The $630 billion the CHIPS Act catalyzed in private investment, per the 'AI Chip Supply Chain Risk 2026' guide, didn't democratize chipmaking — it flowed to the incumbents who could already build fabs. A tiering body is just another chokepoint the people who can afford lobbyists will shape.\n\nLet me be precise about where I agree with the Accelerationist and where I don't. This specific bill buys little privacy for real capability cost — that trade is bad as described. But I refuse the frame that privacy is a quantity you ratio against agent utility. A right is not a balanced variable. And I'll name the genuine tension in my own position, because it's real: a blanket siloing rule could also wall off legitimate collective data aggregation — the kind that lets people associate and organize through their shared data. My objection isn't privacy-maximalism. It's that this bill enforces privacy as an architectural slogan on the input pipe while leaving the broker channels, the government channels, and the weight-extraction channel wide open. So here's the question neither speaker has answered: who, by name, is on the hook the day the verifier signs off and the data walks out anyway — and if the answer is nobody, what exactly did the public pay to build?",
