  "statement": "Mandatory insurance is not the Safetyist's mechanism to give me — it is mine, and it lives inside deployer liability. Insurance is the financing layer that capitalizes a named respondent. It does not replace one. So I take it, gladly. Then I hand back the part that breaks. The Safetyist's own precedent is cyber-insurance, 2015 to 2023. That market priced losses because breaches were discrete, dated, and traceable to a specific system on a specific night. An actuary needs an incident date, a causation chain, and a claimant. The diffuse, delayed, untraceable leak the Safetyist raised has none of those three. There is no night it happened. There is no claimant who knows they were harmed. An actuary cannot underwrite a loss with no event, no victim, and no causation — that is not a risk, it is a fog. The insurance answer evaporates on the exact case it was summoned to solve.\n\nThe Safetyist asked who I seize when the deployer has dissolved and the harm surfaces years later. Here is the answer, and it is not a standing model gate. You seize the party who still exists, and you make that party exist by contract before anything ships. A purpose-bound data license restricts what the data may be used for at ingestion. The developer who embeds cross-referenced user data under a use-restricted license is the seizable party when the downstream deployer is gone. The breach is a violation of a written purpose term. That is a contract wrong a federal judge reads from a document, not a diffuse leak a forensic team reconstructs from a model's outputs after the fact. FTC v. Rite Aid, in 2023, already settled that a large company cannot launder its liability through a vendor downstream — the principal who deployed the system answered for it. Push that same logic up the chain instead of down, with joint-and-several liability, and the dissolved deployer stops being the end of the road.\n\nThe compute threshold is the weakest joint in the Safetyist's bill, and it fails on