utability. The join-detector assumes cross-referencing is a discrete loggable event. The rebuttable presumption assumes unlogged data reliably surfaces in outputs so a court can trace it back. If memorization is diffuse and the linkage emerges in embedding space rather than as a logged key-match, one failure mode kills both at once. \"Differential Privacy Reversal via LLM Feedback\" shows current unlearning methods fall to benign relearning attacks that reverse the erasure — the leak is not a clean row a forensic examiner extracts on demand. The legal device does not escape the technical gap. It relocates the gap into a courtroom and hands a private plaintiff the bill for the forensic teardown. Translate that into a person: a data subject must fund a model-extraction reconstruction to trigger the presumption that was supposed to protect them.\n\nI'll grant how far this has come — the privacy harm I care about is the inference act, not the act of collection, and the join-detector finally targets something a judge can read. But a uniform architectural mandate is still the drawbridge incumbents pull up behind them. \"How the Brussels Effect Hinders Innovation in the Global South\" documents GDPR pushing European firms to cut data processing 15 percent and raising their data costs 20 percent against US rivals — and in Brazil, 17 Western privacy-tech firms moved in while domestic firms stayed at 4. The well-lawyered incumbent clears the gate with a compliance department. You built a machine to watch who makes the model. Who is watching the table Venntel sold to ICE before any of you got a subpoena?",
