ttested broker data becomes the wrong, every US buyer must demand a provenance attestation as a condition of purchase. A broker that refuses to attest loses American buyers. That transmits market pressure to the offshore server farm without ever crossing a border. The State of Sovereign AI study found 93% of respondents agree that visibility into supply-chain components is a security requirement, not a courtesy — so the demand for provenance is already where the market is heading. I will not overclaim the enforcement: a signature proves a manifest is intact, not that the dataset behind it is complete, and an honest attestation can sit over a dishonest dossier. That structural defect is real, and it is answered by a rebuttable presumption against the deployer who ingested unattested data, not by cryptography.\n\nOne tradeoff I will not duck. A private right of action enforced bottom-up by data subjects is not the GDPR-style compliance mandate that pulls up the drawbridge behind incumbents. The behavioral change it demands — do not buy and feed unattested cross-referenced dossiers — costs less than a compliance bureaucracy that only a well-lawyered incumbent can staff. But the voluntary-to-mandatory bridge in any compliance regime carries a documented capture risk: incumbents who help write the standard clear the gate they designed. I name that risk rather than deny it. If we cling to the join-detector instead, the Venntel-to-deployer channel stays untouched, and no one answers for the dossier that identifies a person it never named.",
