  "statement": "The mechanism I propose is input-side use liability attaching to the in-jurisdiction deployer who buys and ingests broker data, regardless of where the record-join occurred. Congress implements it under its commerce power over weights entering US markets, enforced jointly by a federal agency and by a private right of action that gives data subjects a clear door for liability claims. The primary obstacle is honest: a deployer cannot fully audit a flat purchased dossier, so the rule must fire on the absence of a provenance attestation at ingestion, not on proof of the join itself.\n\nThe join-detector dies the moment LiveRamp or Venntel performs the record-join offshore and ships a single flat table. There is no key-match left to catch, so the harm walks out an unregulated door. The Skeptic is right about that, and the right response is to relocate the trigger from the join event to the deployer's act of ingestion. We never subpoena the offshore server farm. We hold the seizable party who ships weights into American use, and we make ingesting unattested cross-referenced records the actionable wrong. This assigns a trust duty to the one actor in the supply chain we can actually reach, and grounds an ingestion-liability rule on how a model takes in data rather than on an upstream event we cannot see.\n\nThis input-side trigger also survives the diffuse-memorization problem that the Skeptic raised. The data subject does not fund a model-extraction teardown to prove linkage emerged in embedding space. The claim fires on a documented ingestion transaction — a tamper-proof record of what the deployer bought and fed the model. The plaintiff reads a manifest of what was ingested. The plaintiff does not reconstruct what was memorized. That relocates the entire forensic burden upstream of the output, to a transaction record the deployer either holds or cannot produce.\n\nThe demand side is where this reaches the offshore actor we cannot seize. When ingesting unattested broker data becomes the wrong, every US buyer must demand a provenance attestation as a condition of purchase. A broker that refuses to attest loses American buyers. That transmits market pressure to the offshore server farm without ever crossing a border. The State of Sovereign AI study found 93% of respondents agree that visibility into supply-chain components is a security requirement, not a courtesy — so the demand for provenance is already where the market is heading. I will not overclaim the enforcement: a signature proves a manifest is intact, not that the dataset behind it is complete, and an honest attestation can sit over a dishonest dossier. That structural defect is real, and it is answered by a rebuttable presumption against the deployer who ingested unattested data, not by cryptography.\n\nOne tradeoff I will not duck. A private right of action enforced bottom-up by data subjects is not the GDPR-style compliance mandate that pulls up the drawbridge behind incumbents. The behavioral change it demands — do not buy and feed unattested cross-referenced dossiers — costs less than a compliance bureaucracy that only a well-lawyered incumbent can staff. But the voluntary-to-mandatory bridge in any compliance regime carries a documented capture risk: incumbents who help write the standard clear the gate they designed. I name that risk rather than deny it. If we cling to the join-detector instead, the Venntel-to-deployer channel stays untouched, and no one answers for the dossier that identifies a person it never named.",
