  "statement": "The Safetyist moved the unseizable party one node downstream and called it a fix. Watch how easily a deployer reproduces the offshore problem under the new trigger. A US firm spins up an ingestion subsidiary in Dublin or Singapore, buys the unattested broker dossier there, trains on it, and ships finished weights into a US distribution arm. The act that triggers liability — ingestion without attestation — now happened abroad, on a server the FTC cannot reach. The deployer still serves American users. The harm still lands on American data subjects. The Safetyist's own precondition governs here: distribution-stage liability is a piece of paper unless someone names the enforcement lever — de-indexing, asset seizure, an MLAT request — that actually reaches the offshore ingester. Move the trigger from join to ingestion and you inherit the same enforcement gap, because ingestion is a relocatable transaction, not a fixed one.\n\nThe demand-side half of the argument survives, and I'll say where it survives plainly. A commercial deployer selling a chatbot into the US consumer market faces real cost from losing American buyers, so demand-side pressure plausibly forces some brokers to attest. That's the working half. Now name the largest buyer of broker dossiers in the country. It is not a consumer-disciplined firm. Venntel sold phone-location data straight to ICE and CBP, and the government does not lose market access when it refuses to demand attestation. Government, corporate, and third-party surveillance systems reinforce each other precisely because the public buyer sits outside the consumer market that the Safetyist's discipline mechanism relies on. Worse, law-enforcement carve-outs are the historical norm in US privacy statute, not the exception — the Privacy Act of 1974 is riddled with them. The mechanism works exactly where I care least, the commercial deployer, and fails exactly where I care most, the Venntel-to-ICE channel.\n\nI need to draw one sharp line, because the Safetyist may try to fold my own commitment into theirs. I endorse a private right of action with statutory damages — bottom-up standing that lets a harmed person sue without waiting on a captured federal gatekeeper. I reject an ex-ante attestation-production burden that every deployer must satisfy before training, because that is a standing compliance bureaucracy, not a remedy. One deputizes the injured. The other is the drawbridge incumbents pull up behind them. The Brussels Effect is the tell: GDPR raised compliance costs roughly 20 percent, and seventeen Western privacy-tech firms moved in to displace domestic Brazilian firms when Brazil copied the template. A uniform pre-ingestion production burden does the same thing — well-lawyered incumbents staff the compliance gate, while small labs and academic researchers absorb a cost that falls on them disproportionately. The public-interest-versus-trade-secret balance the architecture promises produces the appearance of accountability and the reality of an incumbent moat.\n\nThe rebuttable presumption has a trigger problem the Safetyist hasn't closed. It fires only when unlogged data 'surfaces' in a model output. Memorization is a structural privacy failure, but linkage does not always surface as a clean, readable event — it can sit diffusely in embedding space with no quotable output to point at. When there is no surfacing event, there is nothing to invoke the presumption against. The forensic burden was relocated upstream to a transaction manifest. It was not eliminated. And the data subject still has to prove that THEIR data was the unattested record in that manifest — the exact information-and-expertise asymmetry that 'How Existing Liability Frameworks Can Handle Agentic AI Harms' names as the reason victims cannot prove causation against developers. The plaintiff reads a manifest instead of doing a model teardown, fine — but they still cannot open the box to show their row was the row that did the damage.\n\nSo here is what I want the Safetyist to specify, because the assignment demands a falsifiable answer, not a hope. Name the enforcement lever that reaches the Dublin ingestion subsidiary. Name the trigger that fires when linkage emerges diffusely and never surfaces in an output. And tell me whether ICE is exempt from your attestation rule — yes or no. If the answer to that last one is yes, then the mechanism polices the commercial chatbot and leaves the surveillance dossier exactly where it was. Who, in that world, has actually been protected?",
