ntability Act of 2022 already shows the shape of the enforceable version — it treats violations as unfair or deceptive acts under Section 18 of the FTC Act and lets state attorneys general sue as parens patriae on behalf of residents. That is a deputized remedy, not a standing compliance gate, and it is why I back the private right of action over a GDPR-style production burden — the same GDPR that cut European data processing 15 percent and raised data costs 20 percent against US firms without measurably reaching the broker channels that hurt people.\n\nThe single sharpest unresolved point is the government carve-out, and it is fatal to the demand-side theory unless answered. The mechanism binds the firm selling to consumers and goes slack exactly where surveillance concentrates — the agency that buys location data from a broker to track people without a warrant. So the yes-or-no question the Safetyist has not answered: does input-side liability attach to the federal agency that ingests unattested broker data, or does the government write itself an exemption? An attestation-as-purchase-condition regime that binds the chatbot vendor but exempts the state risks recreating the trap where privacy is a commodity you buy in the consumer market and lose the moment a government agency is the buyer. The original siloed-dataset mandate is dead — all three of us walked away from it. What replaces it only protects the surveilled if it reaches the buyer who never faces a market.",
